TEXT 160 0 Left 3 ;#183 The same relay coil with a flyback diode across it
TEXT 160 64 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c6I7Ycbv8B8
TEXT 160 112 Left 2 ;DFLY gives the coil current somewhere to go when the transistor turns off, so the collector
TEXT 160 144 Left 2 ;rises only one diode drop above the supply instead of to whatever breaks down first.
TEXT 1020 256 Left 2 !.lib ..\..\LTspice\Models\Unverified\MPSA42_legacy.lib
TEXT 1020 296 Left 2 !.tran 0 230m 200m 200n
TEXT 1020 336 Left 2 !.meas tran VC_PK MAX V(collector)
TEXT 1020 496 Left 2 !.meas tran IDFLY_PK MAX I(DFLY)
TEXT 1020 376 Left 2 !.meas tran VC_ON MIN V(collector) FROM 205m TO 209m
TEXT 1020 416 Left 2 !.meas tran ICOIL_ON AVG I(LCOIL) FROM 205m TO 209m
TEXT 1020 456 Left 2 !.meas tran IE_ON AVG I(REMIT) FROM 205m TO 209m
TEXT 160 900 Left 2 ;Alan's notes give the drive exactly: a 5 V, 200 ms pulse behind 50 ohm, 3.9k to the base of an
TEXT 160 932 Left 2 ;MPSA42, and 20 ohm in the emitter as a current monitor. V2's value is hidden on the symbol:
TEXT 160 964 Left 2 ;PULSE(0 5 10m 1u 1u 200m 400m).
TEXT 160 1012 Left 2 ;He never states the relay's winding resistance or inductance, so RCOIL and LCOIL are estimates.
TEXT 160 1044 Left 2 ;With the transistor and the 20 ohm dropping 1.3 V, 70 ohm passes 53 mA, inside the range of his
TEXT 160 1076 Left 2 ;10 mA per division current trace.
TEXT 160 1124 Left 2 ;His drawing also shows an LED and resistor on the relay contacts. The contact itself is not
TEXT 160 1156 Left 2 ;modelled here, so that load is left out; nothing in this lesson depends on it.
